WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.401
-We're back with Paul Giamatti.

00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:05.204
You play a teacher in the film.
Your mom was a teacher.

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:07.106
Your father was an academic
who was president of Yale.

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:11.144
Did you ever consider
going into academia yourself?

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:12.812
-Briefly.
It was a little bit of a thing.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:15.114
It was like the family
business a little bit.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:16.516
So it went through your head,

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.717
but I was like, "I'm not cut out
to be a teacher at all."

00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:20.086
I'd be a terrible teacher.
-Well, you're a very

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:21.354
you're a very good one
on screen.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:22.955
-Thank you.
-That's a nice outcome.

00:00:22.955 --> 00:00:27.793 line:0%
So you do have a podcast where
you co-host with a professor.

00:00:27.793 --> 00:00:30.029 line:0%
-I do.
-So I think that's as close

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:31.965 line:0%
as maybe you'd want to get.
-Yes.

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:34.500
And he's a very -- he's not --
he's a philosophy professor,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.103
but he's also a professional
blues guitar player.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.904
He's a very unusual guy.

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:41.174
-How did you meet him?
How did you connect with...?

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.276
-He was he was giving
a talk online

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:46.345
about sort of this kind of
weird brain consciousness

00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:48.847
and the use of psychedelics
and therapy and stuff.

00:00:48.847 --> 00:00:51.217
And of course, I tuned in
to that and I'm like --

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.785
and I'm like,
"This is interesting."

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:54.120
[ Applause ]
-And I -- [ Laughs ] Really.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.557
Yeah! Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:00.526
And so -- And I thought,
"This guy's really interesting."

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:02.862
So I got in touch
with him afterwards.

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:05.898
And he was a little bit
like, "Who is this really?

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:07.900
Is this really Paul Giamatti?"
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.172
-And I was like, "Who would
pretend to be me to try...?"

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.639
I mean, like,
why would anybody go,

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:18.444
"I need to disguise
myself as that guy.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:20.412
Oh, yeah. The guy from..."
-It's funny that the way --

00:01:20.412 --> 00:01:22.582
the reason you disguise yourself
as Paul Giamatti

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.616
is to meet a professor.
-[ Laughs ] Exactly!

00:01:24.616 --> 00:01:27.920
-Like, who the professors like?
-Yeah, exactly! Exactly.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.289
Oh, I guess that makes sense,
actually, now that you say it.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:32.192
Aha.
-He's like, "You're like

00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:33.993
the third Giamatti
who's called me today."

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:38.164
-[ Laughs ]
But we hit it off really well.

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.532
-And how quickly
after hitting it off

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:40.500
did you realize
"we should do a podcast..."

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.934
-A while, actually.
-Okay.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.803
-We were just having
conversations all the time,

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.171
and it was during
all the COVID stuff.

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.739
And he was in Chicago
and I was out here,

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.007
and so we were just on Zoom,

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.441
and we'd just have
these long conversations.

00:01:49.441 --> 00:01:50.676
And then he was like,

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:52.345
"Maybe we should do this
in front of people."

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:55.681
And I was like, "Okay.
Okay, professor."

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:58.718
And so we did,
and it's been really fun

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:00.152
and we wanted to animate it,

00:02:00.152 --> 00:02:01.221
originally.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:02.822
-Thing to be animated.
He had a student.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.490
He teaches at an arts college.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.025
He had a student
named Alex Sokol,

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:08.394
who is a brilliant animator.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.330
Couldn't do the whole thing.
It was too expensive.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.164
-Right, 'cause
they're an hour long. Right.

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:13.599
-It's too much.
So it was like we have little

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.601
bits of it get animated.
-Yes. So if you --

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.770
if you listen to it
and watch it online,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.305
like watch it on YouTube,
all of a sudden

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.807
there's like these
really nice animations.

00:02:21.807 --> 00:02:23.810
-They're fantastic.
-It's really good.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.878
-Yes.
-This is a real reminder

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:27.913
that this started
with psychedelic drugs.

00:02:27.913 --> 00:02:30.349
-I was going to say.
I was going to remind you.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:34.654
Alex has clearly taken
some therapeutic psychedelics.

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.456
-It's really --
It's really wonderful.

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:39.292
-Yeah, and the idea is it's
a very free-form conversation

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:41.760
and you start off
with sort of unusual topics.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.430
You get into,
like, UFOs and Sasquatch

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.532
and, you know,
and it's freeing for me

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:48.735
because I'd reached a point
where I thought,

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:51.670
"I'm tired of not talking
about Sasquatch."

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.707
-Yeah. Because you
obviously have a lot to say.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.075
-[ Laughs ]
I have a lot to say.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:58.110
-But it is a weird thing
to bring up at a dinner party.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.945
-Yes, it is.
-But on a podcast,

00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:01.747
the perfect place to start
talking about Sasquatch.

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:03.082
And where are you
on Sasquatch?

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:05.117
Are you pro?
You think he's out there?

00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:06.885
-I do think there might be
something out there.

00:03:06.885 --> 00:03:08.354
-Okay, great.
-And Stephen really thinks

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.556
there might be
something out there.

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.889
-That's great.
And then does that --

00:03:10.889 --> 00:03:13.759
Do I assume that he's also --
believes that UFOs...?

00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:15.929
-Yes,
he's a little bit more skeptical

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.396
for some reason about UFOs.

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:19.232
-So funny.
-Which makes no sense.

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:21.868
-I feel like there's way more
data on UFOs than Sasquatch.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.336
-I agree, but he actually -- I
think he thinks that sort of

00:03:24.336 --> 00:03:26.139
from a zoological point of view

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:30.176
there could be a kind
of species of monkey

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:31.811
that we haven't
really discovered.

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:32.878
-Okay.
-Which happens.

00:03:32.878 --> 00:03:34.347
It does happen.
-That does happen.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:37.783
-It's this kind of exciting
stuff you tune in for, folks.

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:39.085
-What about ghosts?
Where is he --

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:40.452
-Ghosts. Ghosts.

00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:43.456
I 100% believe in ghosts.

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:44.657
He tends to be more skeptical --

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:46.525
except about Sasquatch --
than I am.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.694
I believe more kind of --
-It's really funny for a guy

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:50.263
to be like, "UFOs aren't real.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.665
Ghosts aren't real.
But Sasquatch, 100%."

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:54.733
-[ Laughs ]
-"And I'm going to catch him.

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:57.836
I'll be the one."
-That's exactly what it's like.

00:03:57.836 --> 00:04:00.039
And sometimes we talk to,

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:02.374
like, an ancient historian
of ancient Egypt.

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:04.443
We talk to
a professional psychic.

00:04:04.443 --> 00:04:06.145
We talked to Kathryn Hahn.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.947
We talked to all kinds of --
-It's a nice mix.

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:09.515
It's a really fun mix.
-It's a really good mix.

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:10.683
-So I have to ask you this

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:11.717
just because
of the timing of this.

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:13.586
So this week,
my wife and I were like,

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:15.154
"We have to watch something
where we can just, like,

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:16.322
enjoy it,
shut off our brains."

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.857
It's a very fraught time
in the world.

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:19.458
-Uh-huh.
-And so we've been watching

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:22.094
old rom-coms.
-Ah, nice.

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:24.096
-And we watched
"My Best Friend's Wedding,"

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:25.364
and then all of a sudden,

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:27.733
Giamatti turns up in the hallway
with Julia Roberts.

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:29.235
You're the bellman.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.504
How early --
How early in the career?

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.706
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.975
-Just look at the --
look at the head of hair.

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:38.577
Look at the head of hair.
-Yeah.

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:41.914
-That was pretty early on
in my -- in my career

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.216
as a -- as a film actor.

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:44.851
I hadn't done much film at all,
no.

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.986
I didn't know what
this movie was about. Nothing.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:49.188
They just hired me
and gave me this scene.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:50.690
I did audition for it.
They gave me the scene.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.758
I didn't know what
the movie was about. Nothing.

00:04:52.758 --> 00:04:55.394
-It is really
like peak Julia Roberts.

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:56.462
Like, you just watch it

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.563
and you're like,
"Oh, my God, this..."

00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:58.697
I mean, she's still wonderful,

00:04:58.697 --> 00:05:00.900
but like movie star
Julia Roberts.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.001
And I would imagine it's just
the two of you in the scene.

00:05:03.001 --> 00:05:05.872
Is it one where
a little bit of nerves?

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:08.340
-Oh, yeah. No, I was
very nervous, but she was...

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.910
Oh, yeah. I didn't really know
what I was doing on film.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.545
-Yeah.
-She was incredibly cool.

00:05:13.545 --> 00:05:15.248
And I remember the first time
I met her,

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:17.316
she was sort of going
through her handbag

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:18.951
and looking for some gum
or something.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.686
They introduced me to her,
and she was kind of...

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:23.088
She turned around,
she had that smile,

00:05:23.088 --> 00:05:26.325
and I was like, "Holy [bleep]."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:29.562
I was like, "That's why
this person is who they are."

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.664
It was just amazing.
-You realize -- You're like,

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:32.865
"Yep, everybody got it right."
-Yep.

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:34.367
-"This was supposed to happen,

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:35.868
and we're very lucky to be here
to witness it."

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:37.069
-She should be doing this.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:39.905
Well,
congratulations on the film.

00:05:39.905 --> 00:05:42.474
Thank you for bringing
our attention to the podcast.

00:05:42.474 --> 00:05:43.476
-Thanks. Thank you so much.
-It's always the best

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:44.343
to have you.

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:46.312
You guys, that's Paul Giamatti.

00:05:46.312 --> 00:05:47.313
He's the best.

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:49.014
"Chinwag" is available
on YouTube

00:05:49.014 --> 00:05:50.616
and wherever you listen
to your podcasts.

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.118
"The Holdovers" is in theaters
now nationwide.

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:54.087
Be right back with Andrew Moskos
and "Pep" Rosenfeld.

